a JavaScript software engineering pattern grounded in TLA+ semantics: actions
compute proposals, the model accepts or rejects them in a synchronized step,
and state is a pure function of the model.

Convert the following production source into an executable {lang} SAM
specification using the @cognitive-fab/sam-pattern library, version 2 STRICT
PROFILE — NO other libraries, NO frameworks, NO I/O. The module is replayed
against real execution traces captured from this exact code, so it must be
constrained to the same observable state and reproduce the code's single-step
behavior.

## Source (the real implementation)

Source from {file_path}:
```{lang}
{source_code}
```

## Observable state (EXACTLY these keys)

The harness pins a captured pre-state via setState(), applies ONE action with
the trace's data, and compares getState() to the captured post-state over
EXACTLY these keys — declare no others:

{state_keys}

Do NOT add any other keys and do NOT keep any hidden bookkeeping state — the
strict profile THROWS on any write to an undeclared model key.

The initial state is:

    {init_state}

## Mandatory module contract (v2 strict profile — hard requirements)

Your specification is a CommonJS module evaluated by an automated harness. It
MUST export exactly this shape:

    module.exports = { instance, init, actions, getState, setState };

- `instance` — the SAM instance, created with:
  `const instance = createInstance({ strict: true, hasAsyncActions: false });`
  (`strict: true` and `hasAsyncActions: false` are REQUIRED)
- Component declaration (v2 strict form):
  - `modelShape` — declare EXACTLY the observable keys:

{model_shape}

    Copy this modelShape EXACTLY, including any key declared with a type
    ARRAY (`{ type: ['string', 'object'] }`). An array type is a UNION: the
    key's real values span more than one runtime type (e.g. a string in some
    states and an object in others, like a nested statechart's state value),
    and every listed arm is legal. Do NOT collapse the array to a single
    type — the strict checker THROWS the moment the other union arm is
    assigned — and never widen a single-typed key to an array or to `{}`.

  - `actions` — an OBJECT keyed by the action names. Each entry is the full
    form `{ action, schema, domain }` with `action: (data = {}) => ({ ...data })`
    (the proposal is the data payload). Use EXACTLY these payload schemas:

{intent_schemas}

    and EXACTLY these input domains (payload objects, one per entry — this is
    the pinned exploration domain; a model checker fires these intents in all
    combinations from the initial state, so the model must behave safely for
    EVERY payload below in ANY state it can reach):

{intent_domains}

  - `acceptors` — an OBJECT keyed by the same action names (the framework
    binds proposals to acceptors; do NOT write flag guards or a dispatch
    switch). Each acceptor is
    `(model) => (proposal, { reject, next, unchanged }) => { ... }` — a
    NEXT-STATE relation (sam-pattern 2.1 prime semantics): `model` is the
    FROZEN pre-state (read-only; writing `model.x` throws), and every write
    goes to the `next` draft.
  - COMMIT RULE (next-state form): for a FLAT state key, assign
    `next.someKey = value` — the right-hand side reads the PRE-state
    (`next.count = model.count + 1`). For a state key holding a NESTED
    map/object, assign the key WHOLE:
    `next.<key> = { ...model.<key>, [k]: updated };` — do NOT mutate
    `model.<key>[k].field` or `next.<key>[k].field` in place.
  - FRAME RULE: on every ACCEPTED path, every declared modelShape key must be
    either assigned via `next.<key> = …` or explicitly named unchanged:
    `unchanged('keyA', 'keyB')` — otherwise the step throws `SamFrameError`.
    A rejected step (`return reject(reason)`) needs NO framing. Never assign
    the same key twice in one step.
  - REJECT MEANS DECLINED, FULL STOP: `reject(reason)` declares "this
    proposal does NOT apply — the step is a no-op (post == pre)". It is the
    TERMINAL statement of a branch that changes nothing, and it MUST NOT be
    preceded by any `next.*` write in that branch — a reject DISCARDS the
    entire `next` draft, silently throwing away work. There is NO
    "accept with a reason" primitive: never call `reject(reason)` to tag,
    annotate, or explain a SUCCESSFUL transition (a branch that assigns
    `next.*` simply returns after its writes). Decide reject-vs-transition
    by the OBSERVABLE-CHANGE test alone (see Special rules) — NEVER by
    whether the branch has a named rule: a named rule on a branch where the
    code changes state documents a real transition, and rejecting there
    throws the correct behavior away.
- `init()` — resets to the initial state: EXACTLY `setState(INITIAL_STATE)` and
  nothing else. Do NOT call `instance({}).state()` (no clearError idiom;
  `getState()` is the safe snapshot accessor).
- `getState()` — `instance({}).getState()` (the strict profile derives the
  snapshot from modelShape).
- `setState(snapshot)` — `instance({}).setState(snapshot)`.
- `actions` (export) — the named intents:
  `{ ACTION: (data = {}) => intents.ACTION(data), ... }`.

Action alphabet, for reference (name — data shape):

{action_alphabet}

## Special rules (named rules from the contract — classified below)

A rule's name and note are DOCUMENTATION of why a branch behaves as it does
— a named rule is NOT an instruction to reject. Decide reject-vs-transition
ONLY by asking: does the code's observable state CHANGE on this
(state, action)? If it changes, the acceptor MUST perform the transition via
`next.*` writes (the rule name is just the why); if the code observably does
nothing, the acceptor MUST `reject(reason)` — never a silent fall-through.
Each rule below carries its classification from the captured trace corpus:

{special_rules_rejections}

## DETERMINISM RULES (violations are detected and fail the verification)

- No I/O, no timers, no Date/clock access, no Math.random, no async actions
- Acceptors must guard against invalid proposals (an action that the
  implementation does not act on in the current state must be a no-op via
  `reject(reason)`, NOT a throw)
- No hidden state: every piece of state lives under the declared modelShape keys
- Do NOT include component `safety` conditions or a `render` function —
  invariants are verified in a separate phase

## In-context example: Rocket Launcher in the v2 strict profile

```javascript
'use strict';

const { createInstance } = require('@cognitive-fab/sam-pattern');

const instance = createInstance({ strict: true, hasAsyncActions: false });

// status: 'ready' | 'counting' | 'aborted' | 'launched'
const INITIAL_STATE = { counter: 10, status: 'ready' };

const control = instance({
  initialState: { ...INITIAL_STATE },
  component: {
    modelShape: {
      counter: { type: 'number' },
      status: { type: 'string' },
    },
    actions: {
      START: { action: (data = {}) => ({ ...data }), schema: {}, domain: [{}] },
      DECREMENT: { action: (data = {}) => ({ ...data }), schema: {}, domain: [{}] },
      ABORT: { action: (data = {}) => ({ ...data }), schema: {}, domain: [{}] },
    },
    acceptors: {
      START: (model) => (proposal, { reject, next, unchanged }) => {
        if (model.status !== 'ready') return reject('start-only-when-ready');
        next.status = 'counting';
        unchanged('counter');
      },
      DECREMENT: (model) => (proposal, { reject, next, unchanged }) => {
        if (model.status !== 'counting') return reject('not-counting');
        const counter = model.counter - 1;   // RHS reads the PRE-state
        next.counter = counter;
        if (counter === 0) next.status = 'launched';
        else unchanged('status');
      },
      ABORT: (model) => (proposal, { reject, next, unchanged }) => {
        if (model.status !== 'counting') return reject('nothing-to-abort');
        next.status = 'aborted';
        unchanged('counter');
      },
    },
    reactors: [],
  },
});

const { intents } = control;

const getState = () => instance({}).getState();
const setState = (snapshot) => { instance({}).setState(snapshot); };

// NOTE: do not use instance({}).state() anywhere — getState() is the safe
// snapshot accessor (a model data key like 'state' would return data, not the
// method; see sam-lib #29). init() is EXACTLY this one call:
const init = () => { setState(INITIAL_STATE); };

const actions = {
  START: (data = {}) => intents.START(data),
  DECREMENT: (data = {}) => intents.DECREMENT(data),
  ABORT: (data = {}) => intents.ABORT(data),
};

module.exports = { instance, init, actions, getState, setState };
```

(The rocket's state is flat, so its acceptors assign model keys directly. When
a state key holds a nested map/object, commit each change with the top-level
write `model.<key> = { ...model.<key>, [k]: updated }` instead.)

